body='''<h1>Database transaction isolation<span class="dot">.</span></h1>
<section class="audit"><h2>Source audit</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-transactions.html">3.4. Transactions</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · Official tutorial · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>The project that develops PostgreSQL explains transaction boundaries through a transfer example. This establishes what committing and undoing a group of statements mean. Its introductory description of visibility concerns PostgreSQL; it does not promise that every SQL database hides unfinished writes at every isolation level.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html">13.2. Transaction Isolation</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · Official reference · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>This implementation reference defines the anomalies and compares standard guarantees with PostgreSQL’s stronger behavior. We use Table 13.1 and the three level-specific sections. It is authoritative for this product, not a substitute for other vendors’ manuals or the full SQL standard.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-intro.html">13.1. Introduction — Concurrency Control</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · Official implementation overview · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>This describes the database’s use of multiple data versions. It explains how readers can proceed during writes, but leaves storage and cleanup details outside this introduction.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-transaction.html">SET TRANSACTION</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · SQL command reference · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>The command reference supplies precise snapshot timing and the syntax used below. These are PostgreSQL semantics; a level’s name alone does not identify its implementation elsewhere.</p>
<p><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.4179">Serializable Snapshot Isolation in PostgreSQL</a><br><em>Dan R. K. Ports and Kevin Grittner · Research paper · Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment 5(12), pp. 1850–1861 · 2012</em></p>
<p>Ports, affiliated with MIT CSAIL, and Grittner, with Wisconsin’s court automation program, report their own implementation work. Figure 1 explains a failure that stable snapshots permit. The mechanism remains relevant; their PostgreSQL 9.1 performance results do not predict current workloads.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html">13.3. Explicit Locking</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · Official reference · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>Sections 13.3.2 and 13.3.4 specify row locks and deadlocks. They establish what a locking read protects, not whether a particular application locked all the data its rule depends on.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html">13.5. Serialization Failure Handling</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · Application guidance · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>This explains the application’s responsibility after a conflict. It supports restarting the whole decision, and warns that another attempt can also fail.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html">5.5. Constraints</a><br><em>PostgreSQL Global Development Group · Official schema reference · PostgreSQL 18 documentation · accessed 24 October 2026</em></p>
<p>The team explains how declared rules reject invalid stored values. Sections 5.5.1–5.5.3 also clarify why a rule about several rows needs more than an ordinary row-level check.</p></section>

<section><h2>The direct answer</h2><p class="lead">An operation can be correct for the data it reads, then become wrong because another operation changes that data before the first finishes. Isolation controls which concurrent effects an operation may observe and which combinations of operations may successfully commit.</p>
<p>The strongest level, <strong>Serializable</strong>, makes successfully committed transactions behave like some one-at-a-time ordering. Weaker levels protect selected properties and can allow other interference. The concrete behavior below uses PostgreSQL 18; the four standard names apply more widely.</p></section>

<section><h2>First, define the complete operation</h2>
<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups SQL statements into one unit. <code>BEGIN</code> starts it; <code>COMMIT</code> accepts its changes; <code>ROLLBACK</code> cancels them. <strong>Atomicity</strong> means the transaction’s database changes take effect together or not at all. That is different from controlling how it interacts with another transaction.</p>
<p>For a transfer, debiting one account and crediting another belong in the same transaction. Otherwise a failure between the statements could leave only half the transfer recorded. PostgreSQL also treats a standalone statement as a transaction, unless a client has already started a larger one. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-transactions.html">Tutorial §3.4</a></p>
<p>To decide whether an outcome is “wrong,” state a rule. For the following constructed example: <strong>each completed purchase must reduce stock by one</strong>. A database can execute every requested assignment correctly while the application violates that rule.</p>
<h3>A worked failure: the decision uses an old number</h3>
<p>Suppose stock starts at 10. Each purchase reads stock, subtracts one in application code, then writes the computed number. Alone, either purchase correctly leaves 9. Together, this ordering loses one reduction:</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Constructed schedule: two Read Committed transactions</caption><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Purchase A</th><th>Purchase B</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>BEGIN; SELECT stock → 10</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td></td><td>BEGIN; SELECT stock → 10</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>UPDATE stock = 9; COMMIT</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td></td><td>UPDATE stock = 9; COMMIT</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The final stock is 9, though two purchases require 8. B’s assignment says “store 9”; it does not say “subtract one from whatever is there now.” This is a <strong>lost update</strong>: B replaces A’s result using a value calculated before A’s change. Neither transaction has seen an unfinished write. Atomic transactions alone therefore do not rule out this mistake.</p>
<p>“At the same time” only requires overlapping lifetimes. The processor need not perform both writes in the same instant. A transaction can pause after reading while another runs; their steps become interleaved.</p></section>

<section><h2>What a read is allowed to see</h2>
<p>A <strong>committed</strong> change belongs to a transaction that completed successfully. An unfinished change may still be canceled. Reading such a change is a <strong>dirty read</strong>: for example, using a stock count of 9 from a transaction that later rolls back to 10.</p>
<p>A <strong>snapshot</strong> is a visibility rule that selects a consistent set of data versions. PostgreSQL uses <strong>multiversion concurrency control</strong>, or MVCC: it retains versions so a reader can use an earlier committed value while another transaction writes a newer one. Ordinary reading and writing therefore need not wait for each other. This does not eliminate conflicts between writers or explicit locks. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-intro.html">Concurrency Control §13.1</a></p>
<p>Under PostgreSQL <strong>Read Committed</strong>, each ordinary SELECT gets a new snapshot. Under <strong>Repeatable Read</strong>, the transaction keeps the snapshot established by its first query or data-changing statement. Both can see their own earlier writes. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-transaction.html">SET TRANSACTION</a></p>
<p>For example, A reads stock = 10. B changes it to 9 and commits. If A reads again, Read Committed can return 9; this is a <strong>nonrepeatable read</strong>. Repeatable Read still returns 10. That older result is intentional: stability and freshness are different properties.</p>
<p>A <strong>phantom read</strong> concerns which rows match a query. A query for available products returns three rows; another transaction commits a newly available product; repeating the query returns four. The issue is the membership of the result, rather than merely a changed value in an existing result row.</p>
<h3>Which anomalies does each level exclude?</h3>
<p>This source-derived table records minimum standard guarantees. “Permitted” means an implementation may allow the outcome, not that every execution produces it.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Adapted from PostgreSQL Table 13.1</caption><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Dirty read</th><th>Nonrepeatable read</th><th>Phantom</th><th>Any nonserial outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Read Uncommitted</th><td>Permitted</td><td>Permitted</td><td>Permitted</td><td>Permitted</td></tr><tr><th>Read Committed</th><td>Excluded</td><td>Permitted</td><td>Permitted</td><td>Permitted</td></tr><tr><th>Repeatable Read</th><td>Excluded</td><td>Excluded</td><td>Permitted</td><td>Permitted</td></tr><tr><th>Serializable</th><td>Excluded</td><td>Excluded</td><td>Excluded</td><td>Excluded</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>PostgreSQL strengthens two cells: Read Uncommitted behaves as Read Committed, and Repeatable Read also excludes phantoms. Notice the last column: excluding all three named read anomalies still does not establish serializability. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html#MVCC-ISOLEVEL-TABLE">§13.2, Table 13.1</a></p></section>

<section><h2>A stable view can still produce an invalid decision</h2>
<p>Can two transactions violate a rule even when they change different rows? This original research figure shows how. Initially Alice and Bob are both on call; at least one must remain. <code>COUNT(*)</code> counts matching rows; <code>x ≥ 2</code> means at least two.</p>
<figure><a class="image-link" href="figure-1.png"><img src="FIGURE" alt="Published schedule: T1 and T2 each count the doctors on call before either removes one. T1 removes Alice and T2 removes Bob. Time proceeds downward." width="540"></a><figcaption>Ports &amp; Grittner (2012), Figure 1, p. 1851, §2.1.1. Original PDF region, unchanged except cropping. <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.4179#page=2">Source and full page</a> · <a href="figure-1.png">Enlarge</a></figcaption></figure>
<p>Both reads count two, so both removals proceed. Different rows mean no direct write collision. The result is zero doctors. This is <strong>write skew</strong>. Run either transaction first and the second instead sees one doctor and declines to remove them. The concurrent result matches neither serial ordering.</p>
<p>The figure is a logical counterexample, not a measurement of failure frequency. A stable snapshot preserves what each transaction sees; it does not by itself preserve a rule spanning several rows.</p></section>

<section><h2>How stronger isolation changes the outcome</h2>
<p>Serializable requires an outcome consistent with some serial order. The database may still overlap work. PostgreSQL uses snapshots plus conflict monitoring; when allowing the transactions to finish could violate this guarantee, it aborts a transaction. Its changes are canceled, and the application receives an error. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-transaction.html">SET TRANSACTION, Serializable</a></p>
<p>In the doctors example, both removals cannot successfully commit as shown. After a retry sees one remaining doctor, the removal condition is false. The transaction can then complete without removing anyone. Correctness depends on repeating the check, not simply forcing the previous UPDATE through.</p>
<p>Serializable protects a correctly defined transaction. It cannot repair a rule the application never checks. If a transaction unconditionally removes the last doctor, that is already wrong when run alone. Nor can isolation reconnect a SELECT in one committed transaction with a later UPDATE in another: the decision and dependent change must share a transaction.</p>
<p>The cost depends on the workload. Tracking conflicts uses resources; retries repeat work; competing updates may wait. There is no universal speed ranking that predicts your application’s performance.</p></section>

<section><h2>Three practical ways to protect a decision</h2>
<h3>Express a simple change directly</h3>
<p>For the stock example, a single conditional update avoids calculating an absolute value from a prior read:</p>
<pre><code>UPDATE inventory
SET stock = stock - 1
WHERE product_id = 7 AND stock &gt; 0
RETURNING stock;</code></pre>
<p>With one row for product 7, PostgreSQL Read Committed makes a competing updater wait, then reevaluates the condition on the updated row. Starting at 10, two successes leave 8. Starting at 1, only one can decrement: the other returns no row. Treat that result as “not purchased.” If recording a purchase requires more statements, put them in the same transaction. This fix relies on this simple row-local condition; it does not solve every multirow rule. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html#XACT-READ-COMMITTED">§13.2.1</a></p>
<h3>Lock the rows a decision depends on</h3>
<p>A <strong>lock</strong> prevents a conflicting operation from proceeding until the holder finishes. <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> locks the returned rows through transaction completion. Another updater waits; an ordinary SELECT can still read committed versions.</p>
<p>For a decision about one existing row, locking it before the read-and-decide step can protect the later update. For the doctors rule, locking only the row being removed misses the dependency on the other doctor. Row locks also do not protect nonexistent rows. Complex conditions need a carefully designed locking protocol or stronger isolation.</p>
<p>Locks can cause a <strong>deadlock</strong>: A holds a row B needs while B holds a row A needs. PostgreSQL detects this and cancels a transaction. Keeping transactions short and acquiring multiple locks in a consistent order helps. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/explicit-locking.html">§13.3.2 and §13.3.4</a></p>
<h3>Use Serializable and retry the complete operation</h3>
<pre><code>BEGIN;
SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
-- Read the data needed for the decision.
-- Check the rule; issue conditional changes.
COMMIT;</code></pre>
<p>Set the level before the transaction’s first query. Use the required guarantees consistently for participating operations. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-set-transaction.html">SET TRANSACTION</a></p>
<p>On PostgreSQL error code <code>40001</code>, roll back and restart the whole transaction, including application calculations. Reusing the old decision repeats the error. The database cannot automatically rerun application code it never received. A retry may conflict again, so applications need a retry policy and a failure path when attempts are exhausted. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html">§13.5</a></p>
<p>A rollback affects database changes, not an email or external action already performed by application code. Keep such effects out of blindly repeated work and coordinate them with successful completion.</p>
<p>Also declare rules the database can enforce directly: <code>UNIQUE</code> prevents duplicate keys; <code>CHECK (stock &gt;= 0)</code> rejects negative values. A nonnegative check would not detect the earlier lost update from 10 to 9, because 9 is allowed. Constraints and isolation answer different questions. An ordinary PostgreSQL CHECK cannot enforce the doctors rule by querying other rows. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/ddl-constraints.html">§5.5</a></p></section>

<section><h2>Choose by the rule you must preserve</h2>
<ul><li><strong>A direct change to one known row:</strong> a conditional UPDATE can be sufficient under Read Committed; check whether it actually changed a row.</li><li><strong>Several queries needing a stable view:</strong> PostgreSQL Repeatable Read keeps that view stable, but does not promise that arbitrary dependent writes preserve a multirow rule.</li><li><strong>A decision depending on several rows or on rows being absent:</strong> Serializable makes correct one-at-a-time reasoning usable under concurrency; implement retries.</li><li><strong>An explicit locking design:</strong> account for every participating operation and everything its decisions depend on.</li></ul>
<p>Ask what must remain true between the read and the write. Then choose a guarantee that protects that dependency. A successful SQL statement only proves that the database accepted the statement; it does not prove that the application’s earlier reasoning still applies.</p></section>
<section class="credits"><h2>Source reuse</h2><p>Figure 1: Dan R. K. Ports and Kevin Grittner, “Serializable Snapshot Isolation in PostgreSQL,” Proceedings of the VLDB Endowment 5(12), 1850–1861 (2012). © 2012 VLDB Endowment. Included for personal educational use under the paper’s permission notice, reproduced in <a href="source-paper.pdf#page=1">the source PDF, page 1</a>. Republishing the figure may require permission.</p><p>Table adapted from PostgreSQL documentation, © 1996–2026 PostgreSQL Global Development Group and © 1994 The Regents of the University of California. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/legalnotice.html">Documentation permission and warranty terms</a>. Inter and Sora are embedded under their SIL Open Font Licenses; notices are embedded in this file and supplied alongside it.</p></section>'''
body=body.replace('FIGURE',fig)
